{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790340600000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:10:25 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:10:25 +07\n     3\t\n     4\t# dao.stream.serve invariant revision — round 2 cross-read (GLM-5.3)\n     5\t\n     6\tI opened every line codex cites (revision:42-184; original design:91-234; `dao.stream.md:263-272, 389, 674`; `dao.stream.discovery.md:70, 159`). One of its findings moves a settled position of mine (Q2), one exposes a defect I missed in both drafts (the ws-identity/peer-id conflation), and the rest I hold or adopt as marked.\n     7\t\n     8\t## 1. Q2 and Q3\n     9\t\n    10\t**Q2 — CHANGE: defer held reads from v1 (codex's position; the earlier consensus restored), with two spec edits.** I revised my round-1 endorsement of Fable's necessity claim, and the directed question is where it breaks: reachability through the meeting peer makes polling a standing cost bounded in **rate** but not in **duration** — the `:max-attempts` budget bounds retries of an *unanswered* request (`findings.md:194`), while a blocked inbox demand is *answered* (blocked) and then re-issued per the composition's cadence policy (`findings.md:137`), which can only be configured, never exhausted, without ending reachability. So codex is right against the word \"busy loop\" and wrong that a *budget* bounds the cost; the truth is a tunable standing poll. What decides the disposition is that **reachability does not condition on holds**: no acceptance criterion requires them (revised §13 items 5-6 need only correctness, not latency; the UCF facade test tolerates poll latency), the coupling between inbound latency and traffic is composition data, and `:serve/hold` was designed additive with both tolerance directions mandated (`findings.md:131`). My round-1 \"include\" was premised on necessity; that premise fails on the evidence above, so I hold the deferral — not to converge. Two edits ride with it (items 13 and 5 below): the honest-costs sentence stating the coupling, and `:serve/hold` retained as the designed relief valve with its enabling condition named (the first deployment class where the latency/traffic coupling bites). Fable's revised Q2 should not stand; the consensus Q2 stands, now with the reachability profile stated honestly.\n    11\t\n    12\t**Q3 — AGREE in substance; one sentence resolves it, as the orchestrator suspects.** Codex and my round-1 already agree on everything operative: the peer's *own composition* mints the id (no meeting peer assigns — I wrote \"there is no assigner… minted by the peer itself\" in round 1), verification is additive, duplicate `:meet/here` handling is convention policy, and a hash without proof of control binds nothing. The residual is only what \"intended format\" may be read to claim. One sentence, which I propose as the spec text: \"A peer id is minted by the peer's own composition; v1 ids are opaque 128-bit random values, and a peer may adopt the self-certifying form — the hash of its public key — so that the later additive verification rule can bind id to key without re-minting, but until that rule exists an id of either form is an unauthenticated name: nothing in this protocol checks key control, and a key-hash id must not be read as a binding (`dao.stream.md:1087-1092`).\" The reason to keep the permissive key-hash form at all (against codex's flat \"opaque random\"): ids travel inside serve descriptors and UCF cells; changing id format after descriptors are in circulation invalidates in-flight references in a repo where nothing else needs migration. Choosing the format once is cheap insurance, and the sentence strips the false inference codex correctly fears.\n    13\t\n    14\t## 2. Codex's extra defects, adjudicated\n    15\t\n    16\t- **Restricted handles — PARTLY true; needs a sentence, not a mechanism.** The need (M reads requests privately while visitors only write; M writes announcements while visitors only read) is real, but the design already has the mechanism: the serve table maps `identity → {:handle h :surface S}` — the surface is per-entry data (`findings.md:160`), and the derivation is \"restricted to S\" (`findings.md:91`). M declares `#{:writer}` for the request stream's entry and `#{:reader}` for the announcement stream's entry while retaining its own full local handles. The edit (item 9) states this explicitly so no implementer assumes the served surface must equal the handle's surface. Codex's citation of `dao.stream.md:272` (attachment correlation delivered to the composition) is accurate and belongs to item 3.\n    17\t- **Outer-gap versus final-gap — TRUE; needs the edit.** This sharpens what Fable's \"the inner retry policy absorbs\" and my round-1 \"absorbed via idempotence\" glossed: an outer inbox `gap` is loss of *channel frames*, never eviction in the final served stream. The inner driver must advance the outer cursor to the recovery cursor, re-issue outstanding inner requests under their existing ids (reads are idempotent, `findings.md:109`), recover inner appends under the payload's dedup (OD-2), and must never relay the outer gap as a `next` outcome — the proxy \"never produces a `gap` the source did not\" (`findings.md:154`; `dao.stream.md:674`). Codex's replacement wording is right; adopt as item 6.\n    18\t- **UDP payload budget — PARTLY true; needs the accounting sentence.** The arithmetic is real (double CBOR framing shrinks the 1200-byte datagram budget, `findings.md:193`), but the behavior is already defined — a value that cannot fit one frame is refused as `:oversize`, never silently skipped (`findings.md:121`), and fragmentation is a documented deferral. The edit (item 7) is one sentence: a relayed channel's declared frame budget is the underlying channel's budget minus outer framing overhead, so inner servers bound batches to it.\n    19\t- **Request fairness — TRUE; needs the edit.** Inbox caps alone do not bound interpreter work, announcement retention, or fanout (`dao.stream.md:674` for the gap honesty side); and a silently dropped `:meet/here` leaves the requester polling announcements forever. M's meeting interpreter needs a bounded request budget, bounded work per driver step, and an *observable* refusal (an announcement fact such as `{:meet/refused <peer-id> :meet/why …}`, or a requester-side timeout feeding my re-read discipline). Merges with my retention-sizing and Q4 additions into item 5.\n    20\t- **Codex's §6.2 identity wording — TRUE, and it catches a defect I missed in both drafts.** Making the ws channel's `:dao.stream/identity` the *peer id* (revision §6.2, and already the original at `findings.md:183, 212-215`) is not vocabulary: two distinct channels to the same peer (ws and udp) would then share one logical identity, and `cursor-mismatch` compares exactly that identity (`dao.stream.md:526-528`) — a cursor minted on one channel's stream would pass identity comparison on the other and be fed to a stream that never minted it, defeating OD-3's cursor-binding rule. The descriptor's identity must name *that channel's logical stream*, with the peer id under `:serve/peer` (`dao.stream.md:263-267`). Adopt codex's wording, extended to §7.1's candidates (item 8).\n    21\t\n    22\t## 3. Merged, de-duplicated spec-edit list\n    23\t\n    24\t| # | Section | Edit | Why |\n    25\t|---|---|---|---|\n    26\t| 1 | §6.3/§7.4 | Punch through the **same UDP socket** whose reflexive address M observed; the udp seam gains send-to-explicit-destination on an existing socket; the direct channel is the attachment that socket acquires; a **nonce-bearing pong**, not the first inbound datagram, confirms the path | A fresh `attach!` binds a fresh port ⇒ fresh NAT mapping under every NAT type; nonce defeats off-path/spoofed confirmation (`findings.md:192`; both models found this) |\n    27\t| 2 | §13 crit. 6 | NAT simulator must be **restricted-cone** | A full-cone simulator passes a degraded punch |\n    28\t| 3 | §7.3 | Attribute a reflexive address only via an explicit association of the request's `:serve/session` with the channel attachment event (both media carry it); a bare peer-id claim supplies no address | The serve layer never inserts attachment data into values (`findings.md:273`; `dao.stream.md:272`) |\n    29\t| 4 | §7.3 | Requests carry a correlation id; on `append-unknown` for `:meet/here`, re-read announcements from a cursor minted before the append before re-requesting | Append is non-idempotent; a new session is a new dedup scope (`findings.md:113-115`; `dao.stream.md:496-499`) |\n    30\t| 5 | §7.3/§12 | Announcement retention exceeds intended reader lag (mirror `dao.lease.md:259`); M's interpreter declares bounded request budget, work per step, inbox count/bytes/idle caps, and **observable** refusal (`:meet/refused` fact or requester timeout) | Gap honesty (`dao.stream.md:674`); bounded state; silent refusal makes requesters poll forever (merges codex fairness + my retention + Q4) |\n    31\t| 6 | §6.4 | An outer inbox `gap` is channel loss: advance the outer cursor to the recovery cursor, re-issue outstanding inner requests under existing ids, recover appends under payload dedup; never report it as the final stream's `gap` | `findings.md:154, 109`; OD-2 (codex's wording adopted) |\n    32\t| 7 | §6.4 | A relayed channel's declared frame budget = underlying budget minus outer framing overhead; inner servers batch to it | Double framing vs the 1200-byte budget (`findings.md:121, 193`) |\n    33\t| 8 | §6.2/§7.1 | A channel descriptor's `:dao.stream/identity` names that channel's logical stream; the peer id rides under `:serve/peer`; distinct channels have distinct identities | Shared identity across channels defeats `cursor-mismatch` binding (`dao.stream.md:263-267, 526-528`) — pre-existing in both drafts |\n    34\t| 9 | §5/§3.3 | A serve-table entry may declare a surface narrower than the handle's; the composer retains complementary local handles | Meeting streams' asymmetric access; already implied by `{:handle h :surface S}` (`findings.md:160, 91`), now stated |\n    35\t| 10 | §7.3/§12 | `:meet/seen` reflexives on a shared stream are readable by all — either state the exposure in honest costs or deliver the caller's reflexive via the called peer's own inbox; peer ids are unauthenticated names until the additive verification rule exists | Regression vs the original addressed `:serve/introduce` (`findings.md:232`); `dao.stream.md:1087-1092` |\n    36\t| 11 | §9/§13 crit. 8 | UCF migration acceptance pins M's inbox policy (count, retention, idle lifetime) for the test duration | Adequate lifetime spans exporter entry **and** M's inbox (Q6 note) |\n    37\t| 12 | §13 crit. 10 | Codex's replacement: same serve/proxy implementation everywhere, meeting interpreter optional with explicit resource policy; tests cover two dial-only peers, loss/reordering at both framing layers, bounded meeting work, announcement gaps, fallback | Merges Fable's symmetry proof with the operable test list |\n    38\t| 13 | §7.3 honest costs | \"Inbound delivery through a meeting peer is demand-polled; the coupling of inbound latency to traffic is composition policy. `:serve/hold` (§3.7) is the designed additive relief, enabled where a deployment class needs the coupling broken\" | Q2's disposition stated in the spec rather than discovered in operation |\n    39\t\n    40\tNo position here was moved to converge: Q2 moved because my own necessity premise failed on the retry-policy evidence; everything else is held or adopted on the citations shown.\n    41\t\n    42\tCONSENSUS: Q2, Q3, defect list\n    43\tOPEN: none\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba collab/1790340600000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r2.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:13:01 GMT\n     2\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:13:01 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n     3\t\n     4\t**Q2 — CHANGE to GLM’s position.** Held reads should arrive with the meeting-convention milestone. I withdraw my claim that the retry budget adequately bounds polling: `:max-attempts` ends a session, while continued reachability through M requires the peer to start polling again. An interval bounds the *rate*, but trades it for inbound-call latency. A held demand reduces wire polling, though it still needs expiry and renewal; bound it to one demand per session, a hold duration, and finite work per M step. This need not gate serve-core loopback or direct channels. [Fable revision:61,68](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:61), [original design:129-131,194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:129).\n     5\t\n     6\t**Q3 — AGREE in substance.** “Composition-assigned” can mean the peer’s *own* composition; no meeting peer assigns its ID. One sentence resolves the format issue: **“The peer mints an opaque, unauthenticated ID with at least 128 random bits; a public-key hash is the intended self-certifying form, but it authenticates no peer until proof of key control and channel binding are specified.”** Discovery’s verified kickoff hash is a rule for *stream* identity, not an existing peer-registration rule; DaoStream currently treats identity as opaque and unauthenticated. [Fable revision:69](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69), [discovery:70-75](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.discovery.md:70), [contract:1087-1092](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:1087).\n     7\t\n     8\t**GLM’s additional defects**\n     9\t\n    10\t- **Public address exposure — TRUE; proposed inbox remedy only PARTLY works.** `:meet/seen` publishes the reflexive address on a shared reader stream, whereas the original introduction addressed two peers. Moving it to a named peer’s inbox does not make it private: the design publicly announces inbox descriptors and the contract supplies no authorization. State the disclosure explicitly, or specify authenticated access or encryption before claiming targeted delivery is private. [revision:47,51-53,153-158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:47), [original:232](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:232), [contract:285-291](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:285).\n    11\t- **Non-idempotent `:meet/here` — TRUE; rereading alone is insufficient.** The draft allocates a pair for every `:meet/here`; an unanswered append may already have taken effect. A cursor minted before the append helps find a published result, but absence after a reread cannot prove the request was not processed or that its announcement was not evicted. Give the request a stable ID; M deduplicates that ID and defines one active pair per peer ID, replaying the same descriptors for duplicates. [revision:149,153,158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:149), [original:113-115](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113), [contract:496-499,674-679](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:496).\n    12\t\n    13\tGLM’s proposed attribution sentence also needs correction: the `:meet/here` **value** contains a peer ID but no serve-session or attachment ID. Attachment correlation reaches the composition; it is not automatically inserted into an appended stream value. The spec must define that bridge. [revision:149-155](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:149), [contract:272-284](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272).\n    14\t\n    15\t**Merged required spec edits**\n    16\t\n    17\t| Section | Edit | Why |\n    18\t|---|---|---|\n    19\t| §3.1, §7.3 | Use the Q3 ID sentence above; define duplicate peer-ID and stable request-ID rules. | Self-minting does not authenticate a claimant or make `:meet/here` idempotent. [revision:69,149-158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:69) |\n    20\t| §3.7, §7.3 | Include bounded, cancellable held reads with the meeting milestone; reissue after session loss. | Indefinite inbox reachability otherwise incurs standing wire polls. [original:129-131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:129) |\n    21\t| §6.1, §7.3 | Specify restricted visitor handles and M’s complementary handles; explicitly associate an appended request with the observed UDP attachment. | Surface restriction is allowed, but the draft neither constructs those handles nor carries request provenance. [contract:272-284,387-400](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272), [revision:145-155](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:145) |\n    22\t| §6.2, §7.1 | Keep `:dao.stream/identity` as the logical-stream ID, separate from `:serve/peer`; provide both meeting-stream descriptors and both inbox-pair descriptors, with bounded candidate recursion. | The current draft uses the acceptor’s peer ID as stream identity and refers to a request stream without supplying its descriptor. [contract:263-270](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:263), [revision:127,135-141](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:127) |\n    23\t| §6.3, §7.4, criterion 6 | Send probes from the socket M observed, support an explicit destination on that socket, and require a matching nonce-bearing pong; test restricted-cone NAT. | A fresh `attach!` binds another socket, and a first inbound datagram alone does not establish the intended bidirectional path. [original:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:192), [revision:160,182](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160) |\n    24\t| §6.4 | Treat outer inbox `gap` as lost channel frames; recover outstanding inner requests without reporting final-stream eviction, and define a size profile or channel fragmentation for double-framed values. | Inner read retry cannot turn an outer gap into a source gap; two frames consume the UDP budget. [revision:55,131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:55), [original:113-121,193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113) |\n    25\t| §7.3, §9, §13 | Bound request work, inbox count/bytes/lifetime, and announcement retention/fanout; define observable refusal, lagging-reader gap recovery, address-disclosure policy, and route lifetime for migration. Test loss and reordering at both framing layers. | Inbox caps alone do not bound M’s work or preserve a call announcement or migrated route. [revision:49-53,158,181-184](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:49), [contract:674-679](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:674) |\n    26\t\n    27\tCONSENSUS: Q2, Q3; OPEN: defect list (GLM has not reviewed the corrected provenance, privacy, and idempotence wording)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '42,73p;104,110p;129,161p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '42,73p;104,110p;129,161p'\"","aggregated_output":"    42\t## 3. Can relay and rendezvous be ordinary peers? Yes, completely\n    43\t\n    44\tThe mechanism. Any peer M may choose to serve a **meeting stream pair** under identities it announces however it likes (a descriptor in a directory stream, a rendezvous topic in `dao.stream.discovery`, a URL on a wall). The pair is exactly the `dao.stream.ws.md` request-medium pattern, so nothing new is specified:\n    45\t\n    46\t- **`meet-requests`**: writer surface only. Any peer opens a session and appends requests. This is M's request medium; many writers, and the meaning of their order is M's interpreter's.\n    47\t- **`meet-announcements`**: reader surface only, single-writer (M). M's interpreter reads its request medium and appends facts.\n    48\t\n    49\tThree request kinds and three fact kinds, all convention vocabulary under `:meet/…`, none of them serve frames:\n    50\t\n    51\t- `{:meet/here <peer-id>}` → M creates two ring buffers, serves them, and announces `{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :dao.stream/identity <in> …}` with the serve descriptors of the pair. Any peer that reads the announcement stream now knows how to reach that peer through M.\n    52\t- On every request M's interpreter also announces `{:meet/seen <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}`, taken from the request's `:udp/attachment` in M's traffic medium. That is the whole of STUN: the reflexive address is a channel fact M already deposits, republished as a value.\n    53\t- `{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <my-peer-id>}` → M announces it. Both parties read the announcement stream anyway, so both learn the other's reflexive address and that a call is in progress. That is the whole of the introduction.\n    54\t\n    55\t**Relayed channel.** A channel is a writer handle plus a reader handle (§6.1 revised). Peer A reaching B through M uses a proxy to B's inbox as its writer and a proxy to its own inbox as its reader. The serve protocol runs unchanged over it: sessions inside, `:serve/open` on B's serve table, cursors verbatim. M runs the ordinary serve-step over ring buffers and never sees a session it did not itself serve. Because the inner protocol already tolerates loss (idempotent reads, deduplicated appends), an inbox that evicts under pressure produces an inner `gap` on the outer proxy, which the inner retry policy absorbs.\n    56\t\n    57\t**Hole punching.** After a call is announced, both peers `attach!` a UDP channel to the other's reflexive address and send `:serve/ping`. The first inbound datagram from that address is deposited by the UDP adapter with that attachment identity; the composition matches it to the pending candidate and the direct channel exists. If no pong arrives in the punch budget, the inbox-through-M channel is the next candidate. No frame beyond `:serve/ping` and `:serve/pong` is involved.\n    58\t\n    59\t**What remains privileged:** nothing at the protocol level. Two facts remain, neither a privilege: M must have a reachable address (a network fact, and exactly why some peer has to be reachable for any two NATed peers to meet), and M must have chosen to run the meeting convention (a composition choice any peer can make). Under the reading in part 1 this satisfies the invariant. It is also stigmergy in the literal sense: peers coordinate through traces left on a shared medium, which is the project's founding model, and the meeting stream is the same object as the discovery document's directory stream.\n    60\t\n    61\tCosts, stated honestly: a relayed message is framed twice (outer session to M, inner session to the peer) and a peer must poll its inbox on M. The second cost is what changes Q2.\n    62\t\n    63\t## 4. Effect of the invariant on the Q1–Q7 consensus\n    64\t\n    65\t| Q | Consensus position | Effect | Revised position |\n    66\t|---|---|---|---|\n    67\t| Q1 | event-medium append; UCF `:put` resumes on observed source outcome; append-unknown leaves the wait undischarged | none; no role is involved | unchanged |\n    68\t| Q2 | defer held reads | **changes** | Include `:serve/hold` in v1, or at the latest in the same milestone as the meeting convention. The consensus deferred it when a relay was a forwarder that pushed frames. With no relay, a peer's reachability through M *is* polling its inbox proxy; held reads are what keep that from being a busy loop across the network. Deferring them now makes the only NAT fallback a chatty one. |\n    69\t| Q3 | opaque composition-assigned ids; duplicate `:serve/register` is relay policy; self-certifying later; public relay needs channel auth | **changes** | There is no assigner. A peer id is **minted by the peer itself**, from the start: a 128-bit random value at minimum, and in the self-certifying form (hash of the peer's public key, `dao.stream.discovery` mechanism 1) as the intended format, with signature verification an additive rule later. \"Composition-assigned\" must not mean assigned by a relay. Duplicate `:meet/here` for one id is an ordinary fact on M's announcement stream; what M's interpreter does with it is M's convention, and which meeting stream a reader trusts is the reader's choice, exactly as with directories. The \"public relay needs channel auth\" tripwire becomes \"a peer serving a public request stream gates it by an interpreter rule\", which the contract already permits. |\n    70\t| Q4 | relay admission is composition policy; lifecycle and caps required; postage future | **moves, substance survives** | There is no relay to admit. Inbox creation, retention and idle unserving are M's serve-table policy over its own request medium, identical to any served writer surface. Caps and lifecycle are required exactly as the consensus said, restated as \"M bounds the number and retention of inboxes it serves\". Postage stays the intended answer for a public meeting stream. |\n    71\t| Q5 | accept OD-2, OD-3, corrected OD-1; amend blocked and anchors | none | unchanged |\n    72\t| Q6 | lease-governed lifetime; retirement as stopgap | none; exporter and resumer are per-migration roles | unchanged |\n    73\t| Q7 | `dao.stream.serve`; rename `dao.stream.serving`; fix ws prose | strengthens | unchanged, plus: retitle §5, delete the relay and rendezvous sections, and rename the copy composition to say \"copy\" or \"broadcast\" so it reads as a convention. |\n   104\tAdd to the rules: **Every peer runs both steps.** `serve-step` answers sessions on the streams in its serve table; `proxy-step` drives the sessions it opened. Every channel, whoever dialed it, is attached to both steps at both ends. There is no server node and no client node; \"serving\" and \"attaching\" are what a peer does in one session.\n   105\t\n   106\t### §3.1 Sessions (replace the identity sentence)\n   107\t\n   108\tA session is identified by `[opener-peer-id n]`, where `n` is unique among the sessions that peer has opened on this channel. Both ends of a channel may open sessions, and a channel may be a shared medium with many opening peers, so the peer id is part of the identity.\n   109\t\n   110\t### §3.2 Frames (delete five, rename none)\n   129\t### §6.4 A channel over served streams (replaces \"A channel over a channel\")\n   130\t\n   131\tTwo proxied streams are a channel: peer A's writer is a proxy to a stream B reads, and A's reader is a proxy to a stream B writes. When a third peer M serves both streams, A and B have a channel without reaching each other, and M runs nothing but the ordinary serve-step over two ring buffers. Serve frames inside such a channel are values in M's streams and M never interprets them. This is streams as values sent through streams, and it is the whole of relaying.\n   132\t\n   133\t### §7 Reachability (replace §7.1 through §7.4)\n   134\t\n   135\t**7.1 The serve descriptor.** As before, with `:serve/peer` the holding peer's self-minted id, and `:serve/candidates` an ordered vector where each element is one of:\n   136\t\n   137\t- a **channel descriptor** (ws or udp) naming an acceptor address of the holding peer;\n   138\t- a **serve descriptor** for a stream some peer M serves that is the holding peer's inbox, together with the announcement-stream descriptor where M publishes the matching outbox: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/serve :dao.stream/identity <inbox> :serve/peer M :serve/meet <M's announcement-stream descriptor> :serve/candidates [M's channels]}`;\n   139\t- a **meeting resolution** `{:serve/meet <descriptor> :serve/peer <holding-peer-id>}`, meaning: attach to that announcement stream, append `:meet/call` to its request stream, read the holding peer's reflexive address, and attach a UDP channel to it while the holding peer does the same toward mine.\n   140\t\n   141\tThe proxy tries candidates in order; each attempt's resolution is deposited on the proxy's event medium. The descriptor carries no authorization.\n   142\t\n   143\t**7.2 Direct.** A peer with an acceptor channel, a listening ws endpoint or a bound UDP socket, is reached by a channel descriptor. This is establishment: the acceptor holds no authority the dialer lacks.\n   144\t\n   145\t**7.3 The meeting convention.** Any peer M may serve a **request stream** (writer surface; M's request medium, many writers) and an **announcement stream** (reader surface; single writer, M). An interpreter of M's composition reads the request medium and appends to the announcement stream. The vocabulary is a convention, not protocol:\n   146\t\n   147\t```clojure\n   148\t;; requests, appended by any peer through an ordinary session\n   149\t{:meet/here <peer-id>}                                   ; make me reachable through you\n   150\t{:meet/call <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}              ; I want a channel to that peer\n   151\t\n   152\t;; facts, appended by M alone\n   153\t{:meet/inbox <peer-id> :serve/in <serve descriptor> :serve/out <serve descriptor>}\n   154\t{:meet/seen  <peer-id> :meet/reflexive {:host … :port …}}   ; from :udp/attachment on the request\n   155\t{:meet/call  <peer-id> :meet/from <peer-id>}                ; the call, republished\n   156\t```\n   157\t\n   158\tOn `:meet/here`, M creates two ring buffers, adds them to its serve table, and announces their descriptors; the named peer reads `in` and any peer may write it; the reverse for `out`. The pair, proxied, is a channel (§6.4). Retention, the number of pairs M keeps, and when an idle pair is unserved are M's composition policy, the same policy any served writer surface has. M is not a relay; it is a peer serving ring buffers, and a peer that reads M's announcements decides whether to trust them exactly as it decides whose directory to fold in `dao.stream.discovery`.\n   159\t\n   160\t**7.4 Punching.** After a call is announced, each party has the other's reflexive address from `:meet/seen`. Each `attach!`es a UDP channel to it and sends `:serve/ping` on the schedule its driver's `now` allows; the first inbound datagram from that address is deposited with that attachment identity, the composition matches it to the pending candidate, and a direct channel exists. No pong within the punch budget means the next candidate, normally the inbox pair on the same M. A symmetric NAT defeats punching and takes that path; the protocol reports a failed candidate, not a failure.\n   161\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '68,87p;263,292p;496,503p;668,681p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '68,87p;263,292p;496,503p;668,681p'\"","aggregated_output":"    68\t\n    69\t## Invariants\n    70\t\n    71\t- Stream elements are data. DaoStream assigns them no intrinsic meaning.\n    72\t- Reading is non-destructive. A reader advances its own cursor, not the stream.\n    73\t- Multiple cursors may observe the same retained value independently.\n    74\t- Interpreters own cursors and decide when to call `next`.\n    75\t- A runtime may drive an interpreter, but DaoStream does not require a runtime.\n    76\t- Closing a stream changes future availability but does not itself erase\n    77\t  retained history.\n    78\t- Retention may be bounded. A cursor over evicted history receives a gap\n    79\t  outcome as data; the reader decides what missing data means.\n    80\t- All expected operational outcomes are data, not exceptions or callbacks.\n    81\t- No operation waits. Every operation returns what is true at the moment it is\n    82\t  called; an answer not yet knowable arrives later as data.\n    83\t- No hidden global state. DaoStream keeps no registry of streams, handles, or\n    84\t  observers, and no operation consults ambient state.\n    85\t- A logical stream has an identity that is plain data, the same through every\n    86\t  handle on it, stable across attachment lifecycles and across serialization.\n    87\t  Projecting that identity never fails.\n   263\t- It carries the logical-stream identity under the contract-owned\n   264\t  `:dao.stream/identity` key. Different descriptors may reach\n   265\t  the same logical stream through different endpoints; their reachability data\n   266\t  may differ, but their logical-stream identity is structurally equal. The same\n   267\t  descriptor attaches to the same stream every time.\n   268\t- It is self-contained. If the stream is remote, it contains whatever is\n   269\t  needed to connect — perhaps an IP address and port. The entry data is\n   270\t  transport-owned; a handler that cannot make sense of it returns\n   271\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor`.\n   272\t- A transport whose attachments are distinguishable assigns each attachment\n   273\t  an opaque, structurally serializable value, unique among the attachments\n   274\t  that transport can tell apart. Every attachment handle the transport mints\n   275\t  has such an identity, whether the handle is returned by `attach!` or\n   276\t  minted from a host connection accepted without an `attach!` call. An\n   277\t  `attach!` success map carries the value under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   278\t  When no `attach!` call produces the handle, the transport specification\n   279\t  names the deposited event or other stream-native mechanism by which the\n   280\t  composition receives the identity. Wherever an answer or event for that\n   281\t  attachment is displaced onto another channel, the same value correlates it\n   282\t  there. The key and correlation rule are the contract's; minting,\n   283\t  representation, and the server-side delivery mechanism are the\n   284\t  transport's. Transports that cannot distinguish attachments omit it.\n   285\t- It carries no authorization. Nothing in this contract gates who may attach:\n   286\t  a descriptor is a name, and a host that can reach what it names attaches.\n   287\t  Whether attachment should be gated at all, and by what, is a question this\n   288\t  contract does not answer and does not reserve room for — an outcome or key\n   289\t  held open for an undesigned mechanism constrains that mechanism's design\n   290\t  from a document with no business specifying it. Should gating arrive, it\n   291\t  arrives as an addition, which the open-map rule and this section's own\n   292\t  transport-owned keys already permit.\n   496\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   497\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   498\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   499\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   500\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   501\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   502\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   503\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   668\teviction until every reader had passed a position would make one reader's\n   669\tabsence every other reader's unbounded growth: a single stalled or departed\n   670\treader would hold the stream open forever, and a hostile one could do it\n   671\tdeliberately. Bounded retention is bounded, and a cursor that falls behind\n   672\treceives a `gap`.\n   673\t\n   674\tWhen bounded retention evicts a value, a cursor still pointing at it gets the\n   675\tgap outcome from the Reading table. The stream reports honestly and decides\n   676\tnothing: an interpreter replaying a log may treat a gap as fatal; a live\n   677\ttelemetry viewer may adopt the returned cursor and continue. Silently\n   678\tskipping evicted history is forbidden — a reader holding a cursor across an\n   679\teviction is told it missed values. This is a promise to a **cursor**, not to a\n   680\thandle: it is precisely what a kept cursor is worth, and it is why a consumer\n   681\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
