{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '45,105p;151,168p;185,197p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.ws.md | sed -n '45,75p;105,135p'\"","aggregated_output":"    45\tthe logical-stream identity does.\n    46\t\n    47\tThe writer surface is on the connection's **ordered outbound path** — the\n    48\tsequence of values accepted for sending on this attachment — never on the\n    49\tserved logical stream. `:dao.stream/ok` places the value in the outbound path;\n    50\twhat becomes of it at the far end is reported there, not here (contract,\n    51\tWriting).\n    52\t\n    53\tOutcomes this transport produces, and why it excludes the rest:\n    54\t\n    55\t| Operation    | Produces                                       | Excluded, and why                                                                             |\n    56\t|--------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n    57\t| `create!`    | —                                              | Absent: the transport owns no stream. Dynamic dispatch for this type returns `not-found`.     |\n    58\t| `attach!`    | `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, `transport-error`  | `transport-error` covers only failures decidable here and now — no socket API on this host, a constructor that fails, local resources exhausted. `not-found`, and the reachability failures that need a remote answer, are displaced to the deposit medium. |\n    59\t| `descriptor` | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |\n    60\t| `append!`    | `ok`, `full`, `invalid-value`, `closed`, `transport-error` | — (the contract's whole set)                                                      |\n    61\t| `close!`     | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |\n    62\t| `cursor`, `next` | —                                          | No reader surface, per above.                                                                 |\n    63\t\n    64\t## The Duplex Model\n    65\t\n    66\tA WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the\n    67\tdesign keeps them separate:\n    68\t\n    69\t- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and\n    70\t  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.\n    71\t- **Inbound**: wire framing and protocol validation first either produce a\n    72\t  valid transport event or perform protocol teardown. The host's adapter then\n    73\t  deposits every produced event — payloads and connection lifecycle events\n    74\t  alike, judging none semantically — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this\n    75\t  boundary. The deposit medium is an ordinary DaoStream stream the host\n   105\ta stream element, where transport-owned keys live under the transport's\n   106\tnamespace. Correlation is by the value carried, never by the key's name.\n   107\t\n   108\tWithout that identity a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence with no\n   109\tdemultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and unattributable.\n   110\t\n   111\t**Server-minted attachment identity.** Before offering a server-side\n   112\taccepted-connection handle to the serving composition, the transport mints\n   113\tits attachment identity under the contract's server-minted-handle rule. The\n   114\tvalue is unique among attachments the boundary can distinguish and is not\n   115\treused during that boundary's lifetime.\n   116\t\n   117\tThe acceptance-offer event carries the value under `:ws/attachment`, and\n   118\tevery later deposited event for the connection carries the same value. The\n   119\toffer, its acknowledgement, the handle, and all later events therefore\n   120\tdenote one attachment. Client-side `attach!` continues to return the\n   121\tcorresponding client-local identity under `:dao.stream/attachment`.\n   122\t\n   123\t**Deposited events are envelopes**, so that payload and lifecycle are\n   124\tdistinguishable and both are attributable:\n   125\t\n   126\t```clojure\n   127\t{:ws/attachment <id>\n   128\t :ws/event      :ws/payload  ; or :ws/accepted :ws/opened :ws/closed\n   129\t                             ;    :ws/ended :ws/error :ws/not-found\n   130\t                             ;    :ws/transport-error\n   131\t :ws/value      <decoded>    ; payload events only\n   132\t :ws/handle     {:dao.stream/handle <handle>\n   133\t                 :dao.stream/surface #{:writer :closable}}} ; accepted offers only; host-local\n   134\t```\n   135\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '45,105p;151,168p;185,197p'\"","aggregated_output":"    45\t- **Every layer is stepped.** Proxy, server, relay and rendezvous are pure transitions over explicit state driven by a composition-owned driver that passes `now`. Nothing waits, nothing schedules itself, nothing registers a callback above the channel adapter. This is OD-5's rule applied to a whole subsystem.\n    46\t\n    47\t## 3. The serve protocol\n    48\t\n    49\t### 3.1 Sessions\n    50\t\n    51\tA **session** is one attachment to one served logical stream over one channel. It is the serve-layer unit that carries `:dao.stream/attachment`. One channel carries many sessions, tagged by `:serve/session`, minted by the opening side and unique per channel. Multiplexing is here rather than in the channel so that channels stay dumb and so that one channel between two peers carries every stream a migrated continuation references.\n    52\t\n    53\tRoles are per session, not per channel: on one channel, peer A may serve stream X to B while B serves stream Y to A. This is the P2P case and needs no extra mechanism.\n    54\t\n    55\t### 3.2 Frames\n    56\t\n    57\tServe frames are the application values a channel carries. Keys are qualified under `:serve/…` with one separator, per the ws rule. The operation and its arguments ride in the `dao.stream.apply` envelope, which already owns request identity, operation and arguments:\n    58\t\n    59\t```clojure\n    60\t;; session control\n    61\t{:serve/frame :serve/open     :serve/session s  :dao.stream/identity id}\n    62\t{:serve/frame :serve/opened   :serve/session s  :dao.stream/attachment a\n    63\t :dao.stream/surface #{:reader :writer :closable}\n    64\t :serve/oldest <cursor>  :serve/newest <cursor>}                       ; anchors as of open, §3.6\n    65\t{:serve/frame :serve/disclaim :serve/session s}                        ; the authoritative not-found\n    66\t{:serve/frame :serve/close    :serve/session s}                        ; either direction, terminal\n    67\t\n    68\t;; operations\n    69\t{:serve/frame :serve/request  :serve/session s\n    70\t :dao.stream.apply/id n  :dao.stream.apply/op :dao.stream/next  :dao.stream.apply/args [cursor budget]}\n    71\t{:serve/frame :serve/response :serve/session s\n    72\t :dao.stream.apply/id n  :dao.stream.apply/ok <see 3.3>\n    73\t :serve/oldest <cursor>  :serve/newest <cursor>}                       ; piggybacked refresh, §3.6\n    74\t\n    75\t;; channel liveness (per channel, not per session)\n    76\t{:serve/frame :serve/ping :serve/nonce k}\n    77\t{:serve/frame :serve/pong :serve/nonce k}\n    78\t\n    79\t;; reachability control, §7\n    80\t{:serve/frame :serve/register  :serve/peer p}                          ; outbound-serving host → relay or rendezvous\n    81\t{:serve/frame :serve/dial      :serve/peer p  :serve/link l}           ; client → relay/rendezvous: reach p\n    82\t{:serve/frame :serve/introduce :serve/peer q  :serve/link l  :serve/reflexive {…}}   ; rendezvous → p\n    83\t{:serve/frame :serve/via       :serve/link l  :serve/inner <frame>}    ; relayed carriage, §6.4\n    84\t{:serve/frame :serve/punch     :serve/link l  :serve/nonce k}          ; hole-punch probe, §7.4\n    85\t```\n    86\t\n    87\tA frame with an unknown `:serve/frame`, a missing required key, or a value outside the channel's portable domain is a protocol failure of the **channel** (decode diagnostic then teardown), never a serve outcome: the serve layer only ever sees valid frames, exactly as the ws adapter only deposits valid events.\n    88\t\n    89\t### 3.3 Mechanical derivation\n    90\t\n    91\tThe server for a handle `h` with declared surface `S` is the apply handler table below, restricted to `S`. Nothing about `h`'s transport appears anywhere; a string-backed stream, a ring buffer and a durable log are served by the same six lines.\n    92\t\n    93\t| Wire op                | Args                     | Server computes                          | `:dao.stream.apply/ok` value                              |\n    94\t|------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|\n    95\t| `:dao.stream/descriptor` | `[]`                   | `(descriptor h)`                         | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    96\t| `:dao.stream/cursor`   | `[anchor]`               | `(cursor h anchor)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    97\t| `:dao.stream/next`     | `[cursor budget]`        | `next` repeated from `cursor`, following each `ok`'s successor, at most `budget` times, stopping at the first non-`ok` | a vector of outcome maps, in order, verbatim |\n    98\t| `:dao.stream/append!`  | `[value]`                | `(append! h value)`                      | the outcome map, verbatim                                 |\n    99\t| `:dao.stream/close!`   | `[]`                     | `(close! h)` only if `h` is an attachment the server minted for this session; never the owner's close | `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok}` |\n   100\t\n   101\tAn op absent from `S` is answered with an apply **error** response, code `:dao.stream.serve/no-surface`: the contract forbids answering a nonexistent operation with an outcome map (Surfaces), and the apply envelope has an error shape for exactly this. The proxy never exposes such an op, so the error only ever reaches a misbehaving client.\n   102\t\n   103\tOutcome maps cross **verbatim**, including the cursors inside them. The serve layer never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor. This is the whole basis of cursor-namespace preservation: the proxy hands the reader exactly the cursor the source minted, and hands the source exactly the cursor the reader held.\n   104\t\n   105\t**Precondition on the source.** Its cursors and outcome maps must lie in the channel's portable value domain. Both existing transports qualify (a string identity and an integer position). A transport whose cursor holds a host object cannot be served; the exporter refuses it before publishing (§9). This is the whole content of the UCF's \"portable cursor profile\": one profile, `:dao.stream.serve/v1`, meaning *cursor is plain data in the portable domain and the source honors it after serialization*.\n   151\tTwo subtleties the contract forces:\n   152\t\n   153\t- `end` from the source is relayed. `end` from session loss is local. Both mean \"what this handle is on is closed\" and both are correct; a program that reads `end` as *the stream ended* will misread a channel loss unless its composition reconnects. This is the same limit ws documents under *Ending a served stream*, and the same answer: the serve `:serve/close` frame from the server carries `:serve/reason :ended` when the source's owner closed it, so the proxy can deposit `:serve/ended` versus `:serve/detached` and a composition can tell which it saw.\n   154\t- A proxy is not a retaining medium and has no `gap` of its own. It never produces a `gap` the source did not.\n   155\t\n   156\t## 5. The server\n   157\t\n   158\tServer state is a value: `{:served {identity → {:handle h :surface S}} :sessions {session-id → {:identity … :attachment a :last-append {…} :held {…}}}}`. `serve-step` takes the state, one inbound frame or the driver's `now`, and returns the next state plus zero or more frames to send. The composition owns the driver and the channel writer.\n   159\t\n   160\tThe **serve table** is composition data, not a registry: the host decides what it serves, adds and removes entries through `serve`/`unserve` transitions on its own state, and the table's lifetime is the host's business. Nothing here is namespace-global and nothing is consulted ambiently, which is the contract's \"no stream table\" preserved: a descriptor naming an identity this server does not hold is answered `:serve/disclaim`, the authoritative `not-found`.\n   161\t\n   162\tThe server is **stateless with respect to reads**. It holds no reader positions; every `next` carries its cursor. This is what lets it survive a client's silence, a duplicate request, or a session that was lost and reopened, without any resumption protocol: resumption is the client re-sending the cursor it kept.\n   163\t\n   164\tThe server never calls the owner's `close!`. A session's `:dao.stream/close!` closes only an attachment handle the server minted for that session; for a source that mints no attachments (a ring buffer, a memory log) it is a session-scoped no-op, and the session ends. Ending the served stream is its owner's act on the owner's handle, observed by the server as `end` from `next` and relayed.\n   165\t\n   166\t## 6. Channels\n   167\t\n   168\t### 6.1 Channel contract\n   185\t\n   186\tHosts: clj (JDK client; server via the existing endpoint seam), cljs browser (client only; no listener exists), cljs node (both), cljd (both under `dart:io`; Flutter web is a browser).\n   187\t\n   188\t### 6.3 UDP channel\n   189\t\n   190\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the ws deposit model and no reader surface: a socket retains nothing. It is the datagram boundary the DHT node already uses, reshaped into the adapter form.\n   191\t\n   192\t- **Descriptor.** `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity <peer-id> :udp/host … :udp/port …}`. `attach!` binds a local socket (ephemeral port) and returns a handle whose writer path is \"datagrams to that address\". No handshake is needed at this layer; the serve layer's `:serve/open` is the handshake.\n   193\t- **One value per datagram**, encoded with the CBOR codec profile (binary, canonical, already specified in ws). Frame budget 1200 bytes, the DHT's figure, chosen to clear common path MTUs without fragmentation. A value over budget is `invalid-value` on `append!` and, from the server's side, `:oversize` (§3.5). IP fragmentation is not relied on; application fragmentation is deferred and, if added, lives in this channel, invisible to the serve layer.\n   194\t- **Declared nature:** `:unordered`, `:best-effort`. The proxy's retry policy therefore has real work: each outstanding request records `:sent-at`; `proxy-step` re-sends when `now - sent-at` exceeds `:retry-after-ms`, with `:max-attempts`, both composition data. Exhaustion is session loss, deposited as data.\n   195\t- **Lifecycle events** are thinner than ws: there is no peer close, so `:udp/closed` is deposited only on local close or deposit failure; peer silence is the serve layer's business (`:serve/ping` and the retry policy), not the channel's.\n   196\t- **Attachment identity** is the remote `(host, port)` pair as seen by the socket; replies go to the datagram's source address, never to a claimed address in the payload, as the DHT already rules.\n   197\t- Hosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs node (`dgram`), cljd (`RawDatagramSocket`). **Browsers have no UDP.** The browser's P2P path is a WebSocket to a relay (§7.3) today and a WebRTC DataChannel channel later (§14); both slot into §6.1 without touching the serve layer.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
