{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"The contract supports GLM’s correction on the polling cost, but it does not make holds necessary for reachability. I’m landing on an optional, bounded hold extension. I also found that `:serve/session` exists on wire frames, while the appended meeting request contains only the peer ID, so attribution still needs an explicit serving-boundary event."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 13:19:19 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 20:19:19 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**Q2 — option (b): `:serve/hold` is optional and additive in v1, enabled per deployment.** A meeting peer must work without it; neither peer is required to honor a hold request, and meeting acceptance must not depend on one. GLM is right that continuous reachability entails continuous polling cost: the retry cap ends an *unanswered* request, while an answered `blocked` demand is issued again. That establishes a traffic–latency tradeoff, not a correctness dependency on holds. The existing design already says a proxy tolerates an ignored hold flag. [GLM r2:10,38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790340600000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:10), [original design:129-131,194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:129).\n\nGLM’s edit 13 is **too ambiguous** about when a hold is available. Replace it with: “Inbound delivery through a meeting peer is demand-polled; polling cadence sets ongoing wire traffic and inbound-delivery latency. `:serve/hold` is an optional v1 optimization enabled per deployment, never a condition of meeting reachability or acceptance. A serving peer may ignore the flag and the proxy remains correct. A peer honoring it bounds held demands, duration, and work per step, and cancels holds on session loss.”\n\n**Three corrections to GLM’s r2 list**\n\n- **Idempotence — HOLD.** GLM retains rereading announcements before retrying. That is useful for finding a completed request, but a missing announcement does not prove the append failed: processing may be delayed or retention may have evicted the fact. `:meet/here` allocates a pair on each occurrence, while an unanswered append may already have taken effect. Require a stable request ID, M-side deduplication across sessions for the retry horizon, and one active pair per peer ID; a duplicate returns the same descriptors. [GLM r2:29](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790340600000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:29), [revision:149,153,158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:149), [original design:113-115](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113), [contract:674-679](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:674).\n- **Privacy — HOLD.** GLM’s alternative of delivering an address through the called peer’s inbox does not itself provide privacy. The inbox descriptors are announced, and a descriptor grants no authorization. Keep an explicit public-address-disclosure statement; if confidentiality is intended, specify access control or encryption as an additional mechanism. [GLM r2:35](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790340600000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:35), [revision:51-53,153-158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:51), [contract:285-291](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:285).\n- **Attribution — HOLD.** GLM is right that serve *frames* carry `:serve/session` and the serving side records a session attachment. It is wrong that both media carry the session ID: the appended `{:meet/here <peer-id>}` value carries neither session nor attachment, and the contract does not insert attachment metadata into appended values. Specify a serving-boundary observation that associates the accepted meeting append with its actual session and channel attachment; M must not derive `:meet/seen` from a peer-ID claim. [GLM r2:28](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790340600000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-mob-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:28), [original design:51,68-70,158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:51), [revision:149-155](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:149), [contract:272-284](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272).\n\n**Merged spec edits**\n\n| Section | Edit | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| §3.1, §7.3 | State that the peer’s own composition mints an opaque ID: at least 128 bits of random entropy, or the intended public-key-hash form. Neither form authenticates a peer until key-control and channel-binding verification exists. | Q3 is settled; current stream identity is opaque and unauthenticated. [contract:1087-1092](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:1087) |\n| §3.7, §7.3 | Use the exact optional-hold wording above; bound any honored hold and cancel it on session loss. | Polling has a standing cost, but holds are not needed for correctness. [original design:129-131](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:129) |\n| §5, §7.3 | Declare narrower served surfaces for meeting streams while M keeps complementary local handles. | Visitors write requests and read announcements; M does the converse. The serve table already permits a per-entry surface. [original design:91,158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:91) |\n| §6.2, §7.1 | Use `:dao.stream/identity` for each channel’s logical stream, separate from `:serve/peer`; supply both meeting descriptors and both inbox-pair descriptors, with bounded candidate resolution. | The draft conflates peer and stream identity and names a request stream without its descriptor. [contract:263-270](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:263), [revision:127,135-141](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:127) |\n| §7.3 | Give meeting requests stable IDs. M deduplicates across sessions, returns the existing pair for a duplicate `:meet/here`, and defines refusal or replacement for a conflicting active peer ID. Keep the pre-append cursor reread as recovery guidance. | Rereading cannot make a non-idempotent allocation safe after `append-unknown`. [revision:149,158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:149), [original design:113-115](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:113) |\n| §7.3 | Define an M-local observation of each accepted request containing its value, actual serve session, and channel attachment; correlate it by stable request ID before publishing `:meet/seen`. | The request value has no attachment provenance. [revision:149-155](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:149), [contract:272-284](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:272) |\n| §7.3, §12 | State that shared `:meet/seen` and publicly described inboxes expose reflexive addresses; claim private delivery only with specified access control or encryption. Bound request work, inbox resources, announcement retention, and fanout; make refusal and announcement-gap recovery observable. | Public reading, amplification, and lag are composition costs that inbox-count caps alone do not resolve. [revision:47,51-53,158](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:47), [contract:674-679](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.md:674) |\n| §6.3, §7.4, criterion 6 | Punch from the UDP socket M observed; permit explicit-destination sends on that socket; confirm a matching nonce-bearing pong; test restricted-cone NAT. | A fresh `attach!` binds another socket, and a first inbound datagram does not confirm the intended path. [original design:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:192), [revision:160,182](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:160) |\n| §6.4 | Treat an outer inbox `gap` as channel-frame loss and recover outstanding inner operations without reporting final-source eviction. Account for outer framing in the UDP budget; define fragmentation or an explicit portability limit for oversized inner values. | Gaps belong to their own logical stream, and two frames consume more of the datagram budget. [original design:121,154,193](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790335900000-architect-dao-stream-serving-spec.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:121) |\n| §9, §13 | Pin M’s inbox lifetime during migration acceptance; test two dial-only peers, bounded meeting work, announcement gaps, loss and reordering at both framing layers, and fallback. | A valid exported entry cannot be resumed through a route M has retired. [revision:158,181-184](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790339700000-architect-dao-stream-serve-invariant-review.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md:158) |\n\nCONSENSUS: Q3; OPEN: Q2, idempotence, privacy, attribution"}}
